    {
      "id": "be-s6-demand-functions-c2",
      "heading": "Linear demand: elasticities from the coefficients",
      "explanation_md": "The **general linear demand function** is:\n\n**Qd = α0 + αX PX + αY PY + αM M + αH H**\n\nThe three elasticities are the coefficient times price (or income) over quantity:\n\n- **Own price elasticity**: E(QX,PX) = αX x (PX / QX)\n- **Cross price elasticity**: E(QX,PY) = αY x (PY / QX)\n- **Income elasticity**: E(QX,M) = αM x (M / QX)\n\n**Example:** Qd = 10 - 2P. Own price elasticity = (-2) x P / Q. At **P = 1**, Q = 10 - 2 = **8**. So E = (-2)(1)/8 = **-0.25**, which is **inelastic**.\n\nFor a linear demand function the elasticity **depends on where you are on the line**, because it uses P / Q.",
      "example_ids": [
        "be-ex-s6-linear-example"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Linear: E(QX,PX) = αX(PX/QX). Qd = 10 - 2P at P = 1, Q = 8 gives E = -0.25.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
