    {
      "id": "be-s6-demand-functions-q-5ce0735b",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain how elasticities are calculated from a linear demand function and illustrate with Qd = 10 - 2P.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** A linear demand function lets you read elasticities straight from the coefficients.\n\n**General form.** Quantity demanded = α0 + αX PX + αY PY + αM M + αH H.\n\n**Formulas.**\n- Own price: αX x (PX / QX)\n- Cross price: αY x (PY / QX)\n- Income: αM x (M / QX)\n\n**Example.** Qd = 10 - 2P. Own price elasticity = (-2)P/Q. At P = 1, Q = 10 - 2 = 8, so E = (-2)(1)/8 = **-0.25**, which is inelastic.\n\n**Conclusion.** For linear demand elasticity changes with P and Q.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "General linear function",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#39"
        },
        {
          "point": "Three elasticity formulas",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#39"
        },
        {
          "point": "Q = 8 at P = 1",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#40"
        },
        {
          "point": "-0.25 inelastic",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#40"
        }
      ],
      "marks": 5.0,
      "difficulty": 2,
      "style_tag": "numerical",
      "source_refs": [
