        "0.25"
      ],
      "needs_check": false,
      "retired": false
    },
    {
      "id": "be-s6-demand-functions-q-8437d466",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Compare linear and log-linear demand functions and show how to read elasticities from each, using the slide examples.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** Both are ways to write a demand curve; they differ in how elasticity is read.\n\n**Linear.** Quantity demanded = α0 + αX PX + αY PY + αM M + αH H. Elasticities = coefficient x (variable / QX). Example: Qd = 10 - 2P, at P = 1 and Q = 8 the own price elasticity is (-2)(1)/8 = -0.25.\n\n**Log-linear.** ln Qd = β0 + βX ln PX + βY ln PY + βM ln M + ln H terms. The coefficients themselves are the elasticities: own price βX, cross price βY, income βM. Example: ln(Qd) = 10 - 2 ln(P) gives own price elasticity -2.\n\n**Graphs.** The slide draws each as a demand curve D with price against quantity.\n\n**Conclusion.** Linear needs P and Q; log-linear gives the elasticity directly.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "Linear formulas with P/Q",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#39"
        },
        {
          "point": "Example -0.25",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#40"
        },
        {
          "point": "Log-linear: coefficients are elasticities",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#41"
        },
        {
          "point": "Example -2",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#42"
        },
        {
          "point": "Both drawn as demand curves",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#43"
